1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:02,090
In January, 49 BC,

2
00:00:02,140 --> 00:00:03,850
one Roman general crossed a small river,

3
00:00:03,900 --> 00:00:05,990
and declared war on Rome itself.

4
00:00:06,040 --> 00:00:08,050
His name was Gaius Julius Caesar.

5
00:00:08,100 --> 00:00:09,450
He had just conquered Gaul.

6
00:00:09,500 --> 00:00:10,930
Now the Senate sent him an order:

7
00:00:10,980 --> 00:00:11,550
give up your army,

8
00:00:11,600 --> 00:00:12,750
or be declared an enemy of the

9
00:00:12,800 --> 00:00:13,350
state.

10
00:00:13,400 --> 00:00:14,470
He kept the army.

11
00:00:14,520 --> 00:00:15,330
With a single legion,

12
00:00:15,380 --> 00:00:16,610
he crossed the Rubicon.

13
00:00:16,660 --> 00:00:17,850
The die is cast.

14
00:00:17,900 --> 00:00:18,810
Rome panicked.

15
00:00:18,860 --> 00:00:19,630
Pompey the Great,

16
00:00:19,680 --> 00:00:20,630
the Senate's champion,

17
00:00:20,680 --> 00:00:22,690
abandoned the city and sailed to Greece,

18
00:00:22,740 --> 00:00:24,650
with almost every ship in Italy.

19
00:00:24,700 --> 00:00:25,730
Caesar couldn't follow.

20
00:00:25,780 --> 00:00:26,710
So he turned west,

21
00:00:26,760 --> 00:00:27,370
marched to Spain,

22
00:00:27,420 --> 00:00:29,650
and destroyed Pompey's legions there first.

23
00:00:29,700 --> 00:00:31,490
Then he came back for Pompey himself.

24
00:00:31,540 --> 00:00:33,270
At Dyrrhachium, Pompey beat him.

25
00:00:33,320 --> 00:00:35,810
Caesar's hungry army retreated deep into Greece.

26
00:00:35,860 --> 00:00:38,010
Pharsalus, August, 48 BC.

27
00:00:38,060 --> 00:00:40,790
22,000 men against more than 40,000.

28
00:00:40,840 --> 00:00:42,330
And Pompey had up to seven times

29
00:00:42,380 --> 00:00:43,330
more cavalry.

30
00:00:43,380 --> 00:00:45,430
His horsemen swept around Caesar's flank.

31
00:00:45,480 --> 00:00:46,410
It should have been over.

32
00:00:46,460 --> 00:00:46,850
Wait.

33
00:00:46,900 --> 00:00:48,050
Then how did Caesar win?

34
00:00:48,100 --> 00:00:49,710
You see, Caesar had hidden a fourth

35
00:00:49,760 --> 00:00:51,650
line of veterans behind his army.

36
00:00:51,700 --> 00:00:53,170
They leapt up and thrust their spears

37
00:00:53,220 --> 00:00:54,150
at the riders.

38
00:00:54,200 --> 00:00:55,230
The cavalry panicked,

39
00:00:55,280 --> 00:00:57,230
and Pompey's whole army collapsed.

40
00:00:57,280 --> 00:00:58,590
Pompey fled to Egypt.

41
00:00:58,640 --> 00:00:59,850
The moment he arrived,

42
00:00:59,900 --> 00:01:00,890
he was stabbed to death by a

43
00:01:00,940 --> 00:01:02,710
man who once served under him.

44
00:01:02,760 --> 00:01:04,310
His head was presented to Caesar as

45
00:01:04,360 --> 00:01:05,130
a gift.

46
00:01:05,180 --> 00:01:06,130
Caesar wept.

47
00:01:06,180 --> 00:01:07,530
So: was Caesar a traitor,

48
00:01:07,580 --> 00:01:09,350
or the man who saved Rome from

49
00:01:09,400 --> 00:01:10,640
its own Senate?
